From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 03:37:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10858/11171] Record an 84-minute heartbeat gap that recovered
 on its own

At 07:35Z the conductor heartbeat was 84 minutes stale, phase iteration_start,
zero children, both GPUs idle, 5m29s CPU against 16h29m elapsed. That is what a
wedge looks like. Checked again 45 seconds later and it had advanced to phase
sleeping with a fresh beat, so it was not one.

The planner explains 23 of the 84 minutes -- it FAILed at 07:35 on a codex
wall-clock timeout after 1364s. The last logged task before that was at 06:09.
The 61 minutes in between have no log line and no heartbeat and I cannot account
for them. Deliberately naming no cause: the observation was taken after
recovery, which is enough to describe the gap and not enough to explain it.

Base rate measured first. Planner FAILs land on scattered days, 4/1/2/1/1/1/2
and today's 1 over about two months, so the timeout itself is unremarkable. The
silent hour is the part that is not, and it is the part with no evidence.

Records what would actually settle it: a py-spy dump taken DURING a future gap,
which separates a blocked call from a long sleep. Everything here was measured
afterwards.

Written because it self-recovered and would otherwise leave no trace at all.

